If this were an element of fluid, there would have been no fixed a even for an infinitesimally small shear stress. Instead, a continuous deformation would have persisted as long as the shear stress was applied. It can be simply said, in other words, that while solids can resist tangential stress under static conditions, fluids can do it only under dynamic situation. For fluids, therefore, the rate of deformation rather than the absolute deformation is a more interesting parameter by itself, since a fluid will any way go on deforming more and more as time progresses, if subjected to a shear stress.


