In the subsequent discussions, we will consider that the range of Kn is such that the continuum hypothesis is valid. Accordingly, we will first develop a formalism to describe the state of stress in a fluid medium considering the macroscopic viewpoint.
1.5 CONCEPTS OF PRESSURE AND STRESS IN A FLUID
As discussed earlier, a fluid is basically a substance that undergoes continuous deformation when subjected to a shear (tangential), no matter how small the shear stress is. The fluid moves and deforms continuously as long as the shear stress is applied. Therefore, fluid at rest must be in a state of zero shear stress, a state often called ‘hydrostatic state of stress’. It has to be noted here that the hydrostatic state of stress basically originates out of the physical quantity known as pressure (p), which acts equally from all directions, and is inherently compressive in sense. At this juncture, it is important to distinguish between two frequently confused terms, namely, pressure and stress. Towards that, we first develop the basic concept of stress at a point in a fluid. Consider an arbitrary volume of a fluid, which can be subjected to basically two types of forces, namely, (i) surface forces (i.e., forces that act at surfaces and are proportional to the surface area) and (ii) body forces (i.e., forces acting throughout the volume of the fluid). Typical examples of surface forces and body forces in a fluid are forces due to pressure and gravity, respectively. Fur


